<!-- Ender IO's nine alloys are now made in GregTech (see scripts/zzz_minespace_integration.zs).
     Disabling the built-in recipes here is what actually deletes the old route: CraftTweaker
     cannot touch these, because Ender IO registers them from XML after CraftTweaker has run, so a
     recipes.remove(...) in a script finds nothing to remove and the old recipe simply stays next
     to the GregTech one. The names must match assets/enderio/config/recipes/alloying.xml exactly.
     The machine recipes (conduits, chassis, tanks, ...) are deliberately left enabled. -->

<recipe name="Alloy: Electrical Steel" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Electrical Steel, Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Energetic Alloy" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Vibrant Alloy" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Redstone Alloy" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Conductive Iron" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Pulsating Iron" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Dark Steel" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Dark Steel, Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: End Steel" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Soularium" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Construction Alloy" disabled="true"/>

<!-- Fourteen more of the same machine, from alloying_modded.xml. Ender IO's alloy smelter also
     ships recipes that produce GregTech's own metals - steel, electrum, invar, bronze, constantan
     and the Thermal-Foundation trio signalum / lumium / enderium, plus their "blend" (dust + sand)
     forms - because it resolves the plain `ingotX` ore names, and those now hold GregTech's ingot.
     Left on, any of them is a second, cheaper route to a metal GregTech's own mixer / alloy smelter
     makes, which is exactly the "another mod's machine produces this pack's material" case the
     owner wants gone; they are switched off for the same reason as the mod's own alloys above.
     Alloy: Manyullyn comes from the same file (cobalt + ardite, both GregTech); steel has two
     spellings because the file ships coal and coke variants. The names are exactly the `name`
     attributes in assets/enderio/config/recipes/alloying_modded.xml. The file's other entries need
     NuclearCraft, which is not installed, so Ender IO already skips them (their outputs
     `ingotFerroboron`, `ingotTough` and friends are empty ore names here).
     Enderium Base is the exception in that list: it was not a GregTech metal when this pass ran, so
     this entry was only switched off as one step of Enderium's chain. `MinespaceMaterials
     .ENDER_IO_OTHER_INGOTS` now registers the metal itself and scripts/integration.zs ports this very
     entry (3 lead + platinum + 4 ender pearls -> 4 ingots) to GregTech's mixer - see section 6ba. -->
<recipe name="Alloy: Steel" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Steel (Oredict)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Electrum Ingot (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Invar Ingot (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Bronze (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Constantan (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Signalum (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Lumium (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Lumium from dust (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Enderium Base (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Enderium (Thermal Expansion)" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Invar Blend" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Signalum Blend" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Lumium Blend" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Enderium Blend" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Manyullyn" disabled="true"/>

<!-- Two more from the same machine, and the audit's other catch: `optional_tweaks.xml` carries two
     extra Dark Steel entries that the mod's own `alloying.xml` does not - steel + obsidian **dust**
     instead of an obsidian block, and the dust-only variant - and both output `ingotDarkSteel`, which
     is GregTech's ingot. The sixteenth-pass list above was taken from `alloying_modded.xml` and missed
     these because they live in a third file; `tools/audit-eio-bypass.ps1` found them by walking every
     XML in the folder. Ender IO's own alloy smelter entry for Dark Steel is already off above, so this
     is the last way the mod's alloy smelter can make one of this pack's metals. -->
<recipe name="Alloy: Dark Steel, Variant: Obsidian Dust" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Dark Steel, Variant: Steel, Obsidian Dust" disabled="true"/>

<!-- And the one the same audit put in the "cheap route to something GregTech also makes" bucket:
     `Alloy: Red Dye` is beetroot + clay + egg -> `dyeRed`, and that name is one of the three GregTech
     fills with its own dye dust (`dyeRed`, `dyeYellow`, `dyeWhite` - the pack has GTCEu's
     `hardDyeRecipes` enabled). Its sibling `Sagmill: Yellow Flower` is switched off in the fifth SAG
     pass below. The other EIO dye recipes produce the mod's own organic dyes and stay. -->
<recipe name="Alloy: Red Dye" disabled="true"/>

<!-- The rest of that family. `Alloy: Black Dye`, the four Brown Dye spellings and the four Green Dye
     spellings all output the mod's own organic dye items, and those sit in `dyeBlack` / `dyeBrown` /
     `dyeGreen` next to GregTech's dye dusts - the same "mod copy in a shared name" case the pack
     handles everywhere else, and the same cheap route around GregTech's dye line (this pack has GTCEu's
     `hardDyeRecipes` on). Switching the recipes off leaves the items produced by nothing, which is what
     lets `jei_dupes.zs` hide them; the names now answer with GregTech's dust. Vanilla's own
     flower-into-dye crafting and GregTech's chemical dyes are untouched, so nothing is stranded. -->
<recipe name="Alloy: Black Dye" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Black Dye, Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Brown Dye" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Brown Dye, Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Brown Dye, Oredict" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Brown Dye, Oredict Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Green Dye" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Green Dye, Variant" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Green Dye, Oredict" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Alloy: Green Dye, Oredict Variant" disabled="true"/>

<!-- The SAG mill's dedicated silicon recipe: sand in, `itemSilicon` out at 50%. The three mods'
     silicon items are discarded and `itemSilicon` now resolves to GregTech's silicon ingot
     (scripts/aa_minespace_oredict.zs), so left enabled this recipe would be a non-GregTech source of
     GregTech silicon - a sand-to-silicon shortcut past GregTech's own chain. Disabled for that
     reason; the name is straight out of assets/enderio/config/recipes/sagmill.xml.

     The three *byproduct* silicon outputs are deliberately left enabled, because disabling those
     recipes would take their main outputs with them: "Sagmill: ClayBlock" (clay -> clay balls),
     "Sagmill: oreRedstone Ore" (redstone ore -> 8 redstone dust) and "Sagmill: Nether Redstone Ore"
     (nether redstone ore -> 21 redstone dust). They now yield GregTech's silicon as a byproduct; if
     that should go too, add those three names here. -->
<recipe name="Sagmill: Silicon" disabled="true"/>

<!-- Ender IO keeps four interchangeable recipes that turn five plain sticks into a wooden gear, and
     its `GEAR_WOOD` alias points at Applied Energistics' wooden gear (appliedenergistics2:material:40).
     That is the *same* item as AE2's own hand-grindstone gear, which scripts/zzz_minespace_ae2.zs has
     just rebuilt around GregTech's saw and file. Left enabled, these four would be the cheap way round
     that - and they cannot be removed from a CraftTweaker script, because Ender IO registers its XML
     recipes after CraftTweaker has run. The names are the `name` attributes in
     assets/enderio/config/recipes/materials.xml. -->
<recipe name="Gear, Wood" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Gear, ae2 messes things up" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Gear, just to be sure" disabled="true"/>
<recipe name="Gear, better save than sorry" disabled="true"/>


<!-- The SAG Mill's ore grinding, switched off below.
     Ender IO's SAG Mill grinds any ore dictionary ore straight into **finished powder** - two
     `POWDER_IRON` plus tin and nickel dust by chance - and GregTech's chain for that same ore is
     macerator -> crushed ore -> ore washer -> thermal centrifuge -> smelting: an ore-processing
     line, not one machine. The SAG Mill was therefore not a parallel route to GregTech, it was a
     route *around* it, and the owner's rule is that another mod's machine does not get to produce
     this pack's materials. Every live ore recipe the mod ships - all 203 in
     assets/enderio/config/recipes/sagmill_ores.xml - is switched off by name below. Ender IO
     registers its XML recipes after CraftTweaker has run, so no script can reach them.

     Nothing becomes unobtainable: every one of these ores still smelts, and GregTech's macerator
     still turns the ingot back into dust. The SAG Mill keeps everything else it can do - its
     non-ore recipes, its grinding balls and its own machine blocks.

     The 40 ore recipes in sagmill_silentgems.xml are not listed: Silent Gems is not installed and
     Ender IO already skips every one of them. -->
